==English==
{{wikipedia|clergy}}

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*kelh₂-}}
Borrowed from {{bor|en|LL.|clēricus}}, from {{der|en|grc|κληρικός}}, from {{m|grc|κλῆρος||a casting lots, drawing lots}}. <!--In Europe (??)-->Many officers at Athens obtained their offices by lot, as opposed to election (Liddell and Scott). {{doublet|en|clerk}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈklɛɹɪk/|a=UK}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-cleric.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɛɹɪk|s=2}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A member of a [[clergy]].
#: {{syn|en|clergyperson}}
#: {{hypo|en|clergyman|clergywoman|;|q3=Christian|bishop|cardinal|churchman|curate|deacon|ecclesiastic#Noun|minister|parson|pastor|pope|preacher|prelate|presbyter|priest|rector|reverend|vicar|;|q20=Jewish|rabbi|q21=Muslim|imam|caliph|qadi|mufti|mullah|muezzin}}
